course: world
lesson: 121
title: The date line and summer time: clocks going forward and back
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Somewhere the date has to change, or a traveller going east round the world would keep adding hours forever. That place is the International Date Line, which runs mostly down the Pacific Ocean. Places just west of it, like Kiribati, Samoa and New Zealand, see each new day first; cross it going east and the date goes back one day, going west and it jumps forward one.
explain: New Year sweeps round the world one zone at a time. Kiritimati, an island in Kiribati, is 14 hours ahead of London, so when it hits midnight there, it's still 10 am on 31 December in London.
explain: Summer time, called British Summer Time in the UK and Daylight Saving Time in the USA, moves the clocks forward 1 hour in spring so evenings stay light longer, and back again in autumn. "Spring forward, fall back" ("fall" is American for autumn): the spring night is only 23 hours long, the autumn night 25.
explain: A builder, William Willett, pushed for it in Britain from 1907, because he hated seeing curtains closed on bright summer mornings; Germany and then Britain started it in 1916, during the First World War, to save coal. Not everyone does it: most of Arizona doesn't, and people still argue whether it's worth it.
explain: Phones change by themselves; ovens, cars and kitchen clocks need doing by hand, or they're an hour out for half the year.
fact: The International Date Line runs mostly through the Pacific Ocean; it is where one date changes to the next.
fact: Crossing the date line going east, the date goes back 1 day; going west, it goes forward 1 day.
fact: Kiritimati in Kiribati is 14 hours ahead of London in winter, so its New Year midnight comes at 10 am on 31 December in London.
fact: Samoa and American Samoa are about 100 kilometres apart but on different sides of the date line, so their dates differ by one day.
fact: In 2011 Samoa moved to the other side of the date line to match Australia and New Zealand, and skipped Friday 30 December.
fact: In Britain the clocks go forward 1 hour at 1 am on the last Sunday of March and back 1 hour at 2 am on the last Sunday of October.
fact: "Spring forward, fall back" helps you remember which way the clocks go; "fall" is the American word for autumn.
fact: The night the clocks go back has 25 hours, and the night they go forward has 23.
fact: Summer time is called British Summer Time in the UK and Daylight Saving Time in the USA.
fact: The USA moves its clocks forward on the second Sunday of March, so for 2 or 3 weeks New York is 4 hours behind London, not 5.
fact: Most of Arizona does not use summer time, so in summer it has the same time as California and in winter it is 1 hour ahead.
fact: William Willett, a British builder, pushed for summer time from 1907; Britain started it in 1916, 9 years later, a few weeks after Germany.
fact: Countries began summer time during the First World War to save coal; today people still argue about whether to keep it.
exercise: What is the International Date Line? | The line where one date changes to the next | The line where summer changes to winter | The line between the north and south halves of the Earth | The line from which time zones are counted
exercise: In which ocean is most of the date line? | The Pacific | The Atlantic | The Indian | The Arctic
exercise: Which place is one of the first to see New Year? | Kiritimati in Kiribati | London in England | New York in the USA | Hawaii in the USA
exercise: Kiritimati is 14 hours ahead of London. When it's midnight there on 1 January, what time is it in London? | 10 am on 31 December | 2 pm on 1 January | 10 pm on 31 December | 2 am on 1 January
exercise: What happens to the date when you cross the date line going east? | It goes back 1 day | It goes forward 1 day | It stays the same | It goes back 1 hour
exercise: What did Samoa skip in 2011? | Friday 30 December | The whole of December | New Year's Day | One hour on a Sunday
exercise: Why did Samoa move across the date line? | To match Australia and New Zealand, its big trading partners | To be the very last country on Earth to see each New Year come in | Because the whole island slowly drifted across the line by itself | To have two Christmases in one year, one on each side of the line
exercise: On which day do British clocks go forward? | The last Sunday of March | The first Sunday of March | The last Sunday of October | The first day of summer
exercise: What do British clocks do at 1 am on that day? | Jump to 2 am | Go back to midnight | Stop for an hour | Jump to 3 am
exercise: How long is the night when the clocks go back in autumn? | 25 hours | 23 hours | 24 hours | 26 hours
exercise: What does "spring forward, fall back" help you remember? | Which way the clocks change in spring and autumn | When to plant your seeds in spring and pick the fruit in autumn | How to jump and land safely in PE | When the leaves fall off the trees
exercise: What is summer time called in the USA? | Daylight Saving Time | British Summer Time | Greenwich Mean Time | Universal Time
exercise: Most of Arizona doesn't change its clocks. In winter, how does Arizona's time compare with California's? | Arizona is 1 hour ahead | Arizona is 1 hour behind | They are the same | Arizona is 2 hours ahead
exercise: Who pushed for summer time in Britain from 1907? | William Willett, a builder | Julius Caesar, a Roman leader | Pope Gregory XIII | John Harrison, a clockmaker
exercise: Why did countries start summer time during the First World War? | To save coal and energy | To give soldiers longer nights | To match the enemy's clocks | To let children play later
exercise: Why is New York sometimes only 4 hours behind London in March? | The USA changes its clocks about 2 or 3 weeks before Britain | New York moves an hour closer to London every spring | Britain changes its clocks twice in March | London skips a day every March
test: Mia: "First New Year's in London, obviously, it's the capital of time." Dad: "Nah." Who's right? | Dad: places just west of the date line, like Kiritimati, see it first | Mia: London always sees New Year first | Mia: Greenwich decides when New Year begins for everyone in the whole world | Dad: New York sees it first
test: Leo: "Samoa and American Samoa, 100 km apart, different days? That's mental." ("Mental" means crazy.) Why is it so? | The date line runs between them | One of them uses a Moon calendar | American Samoa is a year behind | Samoa has no calendar at all
test: Ruby flies east from Japan to Hawaii on her birthday: "Birthday twice?? Double cake, slay!" ("Slay" means brilliant.) What's true? | The date repeats, but she doesn't get any older | She gets two years older on one flight | She loses her birthday completely | She becomes a day younger forever
test: Jack, on the last Sunday of March: "Phone says 9, kitchen clock says 8. Did I sleep through an hour or what?" | The clocks went forward; the kitchen clock wasn't changed | His phone is broken and has been running an hour fast all night | He really did sleep through an extra hour while the clock stopped | The kitchen clock went back an hour by itself in the night
test: Hannah, in England: "Spring forward, fall back? We say autumn, ya numpty." ("Numpty" is Scottish for a silly person.) Does the trick still work? | Yes: clocks go forward in spring and back in autumn | No: in Britain the clocks go back in spring | No: Britain doesn't change its clocks | Yes, but only in America
test: Kai: "Stayin' up for the extra hour, best night of the year, bro." Which night is that? | The night in late October when the clocks go back | The night in late March when the clocks go forward | New Year's Eve | The longest night in December
test: Charlotte in Arizona: "Sometimes we got the same time as my cousin in California, sometimes we don't, y'all." Why? | Arizona doesn't change its clocks, but California does | Arizona and California are in different countries | California changes its clocks every month | Arizona uses the Moon to tell the time
test: Liam's teacher: "A builder came up with summer time." Liam: "Bricks and clocks? Sus." ("Sus" means suspicious.) What's true? | William Willett pushed for it in 1907; Britain began it in 1916 | Julius Caesar came up with it in 46 BC, along with the leap year | Samoa came up with it in 2011, on the day it skipped a whole Friday | A builder made the very first clock in 1907 and called it summer time
test: Sam's dad in mid-March: "Bloody New York's changed their clocks before us again." What has happened? | The USA moved its clocks forward on the second Sunday of March, before Britain | New York has moved itself into a different time zone for good, an hour nearer London | Britain moved its clocks back in March | New York gave up summer time
test: Nan, about her oven clock: "I just add an hour in me head, love." In July the oven says 2:00. What's the real time? | 3:00 | 1:00 | 2:00 | 4:00
test: What would happen if Britain kept summer time all winter long? | Winter mornings would be darker, with sunrise later by the clock | Winter days would become an hour longer | The Sun would rise an hour earlier by the clock | Nothing at all, since clocks don't affect anything
test: A film shows a hero crossing the date line going west at 11 pm on Monday. What date is it on the other side? | Tuesday, because going west the date jumps forward | Sunday, because going west the date always goes back | Still Monday, because dates never change out at sea | The next Monday, a whole week later on the calendar
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the crossing of the International Date Line in an easterly direction. | The date is set back by one day | The date is advanced by one day | The clock alone is set back by one hour | Neither date nor clock is altered
exam: Given that Kiritimati is fourteen hours ahead of London, ascertain the time in London at the moment Kiritimati attains midnight commencing the first of January. | Ten o'clock in the morning of the thirty-first of December | Two o'clock in the afternoon of the first of January | Ten o'clock in the evening of the thirty-first of December | Midnight of the thirty-first of December
exam: Identify the duration of the night upon which British clocks are put back by one hour in October. | Twenty-five hours | Twenty-three hours | Twenty-four hours | Twenty-six hours
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of summer time in Britain? | It was advocated from 1907 by William Willett and adopted in 1916 | It was decreed by Pope Gregory the Thirteenth for all Christendom in 1582 | It was adopted in 1752 together with the Gregorian calendar in Britain | It was devised in 1884 at the International Meridian Conference in Washington
